    "proj_quin_cat": "Client project · Management web app",
    "proj_quin_title": "Alpha Quincaillerie",
    "proj_quin_desc": "The hardware store edition of Alpha for Amirson Quincaillerie: a till that sells by the metre, the litre or the roll, stock you count the way you do on the shelf, and invoices ready for the shop stamp.",
    "quin_m_year": "2026",
    "quin_m_role": "Project lead, design and development",
    "quin_m_type": "Client project",
    "quin_m_client": "Amirson Quincaillerie",
    "quin_m_tools": "React, Vite, Tailwind, Supabase, Vercel",
    "quin_m_status": "Approved, goes live after the stock count",
    "quin_s_context": "<p>After Alpha V2 went live at Amirson Machine, the family's second shop, Amirson Quincaillerie, wanted the same tool. But a hardware store doesn't sell like a machine shop: the same cable goes out <strong>by the metre or by the roll</strong>, paint <strong>by the litre or by the bucket</strong>, nails <strong>by the kilo or by the box</strong>. For now the owner runs the counter himself, and a seller may join him later.</p>",
    "quin_s_problem": "<p>How do you keep one accurate stock when an item sometimes leaves by the metre and sometimes by the roll, without turning every sale into a conversion exercise, and without losing a sale when the internet drops?</p>",
    "quin_s_solution": "<p>I took the Alpha product and reworked the data model around units:</p><ul><li><strong>A base unit and packagings</strong>: stock is counted in the smallest unit sold, and each packaging has its own price. Selling 2 rolls removes 200 m, and stock reads in plain words: “3 rolls + 25 m”.</li><li><strong>A till built for the counter</strong>: catalogue on the left, ticket on the right, and each item offers its selling options right away (by the metre, 100 m roll). Change due and the amount on credit are worked out automatically.</li><li><strong>Sales calculated by the database</strong>: a PostgreSQL function records the sale, the profit and the stock movement in a single transaction. A sale made offline and sent once the network is back never creates a duplicate.</li><li><strong>One app, two roles</strong>: the owner sees everything, the seller never sees purchase prices or profits. It isn't a hidden screen, the database itself refuses (RLS rules).</li><li><strong>Deliveries and stock counts</strong>: a supplier delivery note raises the stock and updates the purchase price, and the stock count works the way you count on the shelf, in mixed units.</li><li><strong>Direct printing</strong>: A4, A5, receipt or duplicate invoices straight from the browser, plus an Excel import of the catalogue for stock count day.</li></ul>",
    "quin_s_results": "<ul><li>Approved by the client, going live after the stock count</li><li>44 automated database tests: units, permissions, credit, cancellations, offline sales</li><li>10 screens: till, documents, credit, stock, dashboard, products, deliveries, team, activity log, exports</li><li>One app instead of the two used in the Amirson Machine version</li><li>A dark “Workshop” interface built for the counter, with a light theme for bright sunlight</li></ul>",
    "quin_s_learned": "<p>The real topic wasn't the screen but the model: a quantity only means something with its unit. Picking a base unit and conversion factors, then letting the database do the maths, is what keeps the stock right whichever way you sell. I also learned not to assume anything about an online service: new Supabase projects no longer expose tables to the API by default, and it was a carefully read error message, not a hunch, that showed it.</p>",
