    "proj_alb_cat": "Client project · Business web app",
    "proj_alb_title": "ETS AL BARAKAT",
    "proj_alb_desc": "Point of sale, customer credit, inventory and stock counts for a wholesale grocery shop in Lomé. Built for the phone, it keeps selling when the power goes out.",
    "alb_m_year": "2026",
    "alb_m_role": "Project lead, design and development",
    "alb_m_type": "Client project",
    "alb_m_client": "ETS AL BARAKAT, Amlacopé (Lomé)",
    "alb_m_tools": "React, TypeScript, Supabase, Vercel",
    "alb_m_status": "Delivered (v1.0)",
    "alb_s_context": "<p>ETS AL BARAKAT is a grocery shop that mostly sells wholesale: rice by the bag, oil by the carton, pasta by the box. The manager works with an assistant, takes cash, T-Money or Flooz, and gives credit to trusted customers.</p><p>Instead of an interview, I had her fill in an online questionnaire. Her answers shaped everything: her <strong>number one fear</strong> was losing control of what leaves the shop, her <strong>priority</strong> was making stock counts easy.</p>",
    "alb_s_problem": "<p>How do you give the manager control over stock and cash in front of her assistant, with no barcode scanner, and with power cuts that regularly switch off the internet box?</p>",
    "alb_s_solution": "<p>Every questionnaire answer became a decision:</p><ul><li><strong>A till that does the maths</strong>: carton, box or tiered prices (“150 F from 3”), change due or balance on credit, split payments in cash and T-Money, receipt sent over WhatsApp.</li><li><strong>Two roles, clear limits</strong>: the assistant sells but never sees the day's total or the stock value. Out of stock, credit limit exceeded, price changed or free item: she is blocked until the manager enters her code.</li><li><strong>Credit like a notebook</strong>: each customer has an account, a limit, partial repayments and a one-tap WhatsApp reminder.</li><li><strong>Stock under control</strong>: deliveries update the purchase price, losses, breakage and theft are logged with a reason, counts are done by the assistant and approved by the manager.</li><li><strong>A blind end-of-day cash count</strong>: the assistant counts the drawer without seeing the expected amount, the manager then compares each payment method.</li><li><strong>It sells even offline</strong>: sales stay on the device and are sent automatically when the network returns, never twice.</li><li><strong>Invoices ready to go</strong>: cut-in-half duplicate or full A4, over several pages if needed, with the amount written in words.</li></ul>",
    "alb_s_results": "<ul><li>App delivered as v1.0, installed on the manager's computer and phone</li><li>Before go-live, a tester outside the project attacked the app: 19 issues found, all fixed and rechecked</li><li>Updates without reinstalling anything: the app shows “A new version is ready”</li><li>Free hosting, with the database woken up automatically every morning</li></ul>",
    "alb_s_learned": "<p>Starting from the client's fear rather than a feature list changed everything: stock control became the thread of the whole app, from the unlock code to the blind cash count. And having someone else attack my work before delivery showed me 19 flaws I would not have found alone.</p>",
