    "antre_m_year": "August to October 2026",
    "antre_m_role": "Design and development",
    "antre_m_type": "Side project",
    "antre_m_client": "My group of friends",
    "antre_m_tools": "Next.js, Supabase, Cloudinary, Vercel",
    "antre_m_status": "Live, used by the group",
    "antre_s_context": "<p>After every outing with friends, the photos scatter: some on WhatsApp, some on Snapchat, most stay on people's phones. Three weeks later, nobody can find the beach photo or remember who was there. The group's memories had no home.</p><p>Before coding, I validated the idea: I pitched it to the group, then had them try an HTML prototype on their phones. They were in, so I started the real build.</p>",
    "antre_s_problem": "<p>How do you build a private place for a single group, pleasant enough that people want to come back, without letting just anyone in, and with zero budget?</p>",
    "antre_s_solution": "<p>An installable web app, designed for the phone first:</p><ul><li><strong>Joining the group</strong>: a single-use invite code, then a 4-step sign-up that ends on a numbered member card under confetti.</li><li><strong>Creating an outing in seconds</strong>: the date in one tap (“Today”, “Yesterday”, “Last Saturday”), known places suggested, attendees ticked by tapping their face.</li><li><strong>One album per outing</strong>: cover, “who was there”, free emoji reactions and comments on every photo. Those who missed it still see everything, and the album downloads as a .zip or straight to the phone's gallery.</li><li><strong>The feed and the bell</strong>: recent albums, a memory of the day drawn from older photos, and a bell that only lists what concerns you.</li><li><strong>Real-time group chat</strong>: instant messages, “Marco is typing…”, who's online, reactions with a long press.</li><li><strong>A real app without a store</strong>: installable on the home screen, push notifications even when closed (iPhone included), a clean offline page, light or dark theme.</li></ul>",
    "antre_s_results": "<ul><li>Live and used by my group of friends, on a 100% free stack (Vercel, Supabase, Cloudinary)</li><li>Code audited before opening up: guessable invite codes, one code creating several accounts, a member able to make himself admin, public sign-up left open. All fixed</li><li>Invite codes now 8 characters, valid for 7 days, with limited attempts, and access rules (RLS) on every table</li><li>Automatic wake-up every morning so the free database never falls asleep again</li></ul>",
    "antre_s_learned": "<p>A cheap prototype kept me from building the wrong thing. Having fresh eyes review my code let me find the flaws before anyone else did. And emotional details matter as much as features: the member card did more for adoption than any setting.</p>",
